<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan III was Grand Prince of Moscow in the late fifteenth century, often called Ivan the Great.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan IV of Russia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan IV, known as Ivan the Terrible, was the first ruler of Muscovy to be crowned tsar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-ivanovich-of-ryazan"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Ivanovich of Ryazan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Ivanovich was the last grand prince of Ryazan, ruling from 1500.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mazepa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-mazepa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Mazepa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Mazepa was hetman of the Cossack Hetmanate from 1687 to 1708 and a generous patron of Orthodox churches, learning and the Kyiv-Mohyla Academy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mazuranic"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-mazuranic.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Mažuranić</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Mažuranić was a Croatian poet, lawyer and politician who served as ban of Croatia from 1873 to 1880, the first ban not of noble birth.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mestrovic"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-mestrovic.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Meštrović</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Meštrović was a Croatian sculptor and architect, one of the leading sculptors of the twentieth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-paskevich"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-paskevich.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Paskevich</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Paskevich was a Russian field marshal who won the wars against Persia in 1826–1828 and the Ottoman Empire in 1828–1829.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-shishman"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-shishman.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Shishman</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Shishman was the last tsar of Bulgaria at Tarnovo, reigning from 1371 to 1395.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-sorokin"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Sorokin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Sorokin was a Kuban Cossack officer and Red commander in the Russian Civil War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-sratsimir"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Sratsimir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Sratsimir was tsar of Bulgaria at Vidin from 1356 to 1396.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-subasic"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-subasic.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Šubašić</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Šubašić was a Croatian politician of the Croatian Peasant