i class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/izmail"><span class="enc-name">Izmail</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Izmail is a port town in the Odesa region of Ukraine on the Kiliya branch of the Danube delta.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/izmit"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/izmit.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">İzmit</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">İzmit is a city in north-western Turkey at the head of the Gulf of İzmit on the Sea of Marmara.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/iztaccihuatl"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/iztaccihuatl.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Iztaccíhuatl</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Iztaccíhuatl is a dormant volcano in central Mexico, at 5,230 metres the country's third-highest mountain, on the border of the states of Mexico and Puebla.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/izu-province"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/izu-province.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Izu Province</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Izu Province was an old province of Japan covering the Izu Peninsula and the Izu Islands, now part of Shizuoka Prefecture and Tokyo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/izumo-province"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/izumo-province.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Izumo Province</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Izumo Province was an old province of Japan on the Sea of Japan coast, in the east of modern Shimane Prefecture.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/izyaslav-of-polotsk"><span class="enc-name">Izyaslav of Polotsk</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Izyaslav was a son of Vladimir the Great of Kiev and Rogneda of Polotsk, who was granted Polotsk, his mother's homeland, around 987.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-dawla"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/izz-al-dawla.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Izz al-Dawla</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Izz al-Dawla Bakhtiyar was the Buyid ruler of Iraq from 967 to 978, succeeding his father Mu'izz al-Dawla.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-din-masud-i"><span class="enc-name">Izz al-Din Mas'ud I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Izz al-Din Mas'ud I was the Zengid atabeg of Mosul from 1180 to 1193, a nephew of Nur al-Din.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-din-saltuk-ii"><span class="enc-name">Izz al-Din Saltuk II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Izz al-Din Saltuk II was ruler of the Saltukids, a Turkish dynasty based at Erzurum in eastern Anatolia, in the mid-12th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-din-shir"><span class="enc-name">Izz al-Din Shir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Izz al-Din Shir was a Kurdish emir of Hakkari in the late 14th century, ruling from Julamerk in the mountains of south-eastern Anatolia.</span></a></li></ul>
